    "created_at": "2025-03-31T14:57:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-03-31T14:57:41Z",
    "body": "@annevk @sisidovski I've been thinking about this. Is their any use cases ? I mean is their any URL schemes host outside the special schemes, which will need to be processed as opaque host ?\r\n\r\nMost of URL-like URIs (`mailto`, `irc`... and pretty much every custom scheme) expected their host to be IDNA processed because they rely on DNS. So let's say we want to create an URLPattern for a mail server with [non-ASCII char hostname](https://mailoji.com), it's still better to process the hostname as non-opaque for better matching. Furthermore they need to be explicited init from a struct\r\n\r\nDo you know of cases were opaque host will be more suitable ? The advantage over non-opaque is non-desctructive (percent) encoding of the hostname, maybe that's required in some cases ?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
